-- Idea cards, batch 2 (Nov 1 2026): 74 more cards (100 total) on psychology, green tech, exploration,
-- biology, mining and interesting start-ups. Same rules as batch 1: summaries in our own words, credited,
-- each ending with a question to start a conversation. No direct quotes.
-- Adds four interest topics so these cards can match people: Psychology, Biology, Mining, Exploration.
-- Safe to re-run.

insert into public.topics (slug, name)
select v.slug, v.name
from (values ('psychology','Psychology'), ('biology','Biology'), ('mining','Mining'), ('exploration','Exploration')) v(slug, name)
where not exists (select 1 from public.topics t where t.slug = v.slug or lower(t.name) = lower(v.name));

select public.put_idea_cards($json$[

{"title":"Small talk with strangers beats the silent commute","source_title":"Mistakenly Seeking Solitude (study)","source_author":"Nicholas Epley and Juliana Schroeder","topics":["psychology","community"],
 "body":"Commuters were asked to talk to a stranger, sit in silence, or do whatever they usually do. Most expected talking to be awkward, yet the talkers had the most pleasant ride. We underestimate how much others want to connect. When did a random chat with a stranger make your day?"},
{"title":"They liked you more than you think","source_title":"The Liking Gap (study)","source_author":"Erica Boothby and colleagues","topics":["psychology","networking","community"],
 "body":"After meeting someone new, people consistently underestimate how much the other person liked them and enjoyed the conversation. The gap can last for months. That second message you're hesitating to send is probably welcome. Who should you follow up with?"},
{"title":"Nobody noticed your shirt as much as you did","source_title":"The Spotlight Effect (study)","source_author":"Thomas Gilovich and colleagues","topics":["psychology","networking"],
 "body":"Students wore an embarrassing T-shirt into a room and guessed how many people noticed. Far fewer did than they expected. We feel like everyone is watching our mistakes, but most people are busy worrying about themselves. What would you try if you knew nobody was really watching?"},
{"title":"Relationships are the best predictor of a good life","source_title":"The Good Life","source_author":"Robert Waldinger and Marc Schulz","topics":["psychology","community","meaning"],
 "body":"Harvard has followed the same group of people, and later their children, for more than 80 years. The clearest finding: warm relationships predicted health and happiness in old age better than money, fame or cholesterol. Connection is a health habit. Who do you need to see more often?"},
{"title":"Growth mindset: skills are built, not handed out","source_title":"Mindset","source_author":"Carol S. Dweck","topics":["psychology","coaching-youth","leadership"],
 "body":"People who see ability as fixed avoid challenges that might expose them. People who see it as something you grow treat failure as feedback. Praising effort and strategy, not raw talent, helps kids and teams take on harder things. Where did you get better by struggling first?"},
{"title":"Flow happens at the edge of your skill","source_title":"Flow","source_author":"Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi","topics":["psychology","meaning"],
 "body":"Those hours that disappear while you're fully absorbed tend to share a pattern: a clear goal, quick feedback, and a challenge just above your current skill. Too easy is boring, too hard is stressful. Climbers, surgeons and coders all describe it. What puts you in flow?"},
{"title":"Change the routine, keep the cue and reward","source_title":"The Power of Habit","source_author":"Charles Duhigg","topics":["psychology","fitness"],
 "body":"Habits run on a loop: a cue triggers a routine that leads to a reward. Fighting the craving rarely works. Spot the cue, keep the reward, and swap in a better routine, like a walk instead of a snack when the 3 pm slump hits. What habit loop would you like to rewire?"},
{"title":"The decoy that makes you pick the bundle","source_title":"Predictably Irrational","source_author":"Dan Ariely","topics":["psychology","marketing"],
 "body":"Offer online-only, print-only, or print plus online, with print-only priced the same as the bundle. Nobody picks print-only, but its presence makes the bundle look like a steal, and far more people choose it. Our choices bend to what's next to them. Where have you seen a decoy?"},
{"title":"We're bad at predicting what will make us happy","source_title":"Stumbling on Happiness","source_author":"Daniel Gilbert","topics":["psychology","meaning"],
 "body":"We overestimate how good the promotion or how bad the breakup will feel, and for how long. A better forecast than your imagination: ask people who are already living the thing you want. What's something you were sure would change everything that didn't?"},
{"title":"The rider and the elephant","source_title":"The Happiness Hypothesis","source_author":"Jonathan Haidt","topics":["psychology","philosophy"],
 "body":"Our reasoning mind is a small rider on a big emotional elephant. The rider can steer a little, but if the elephant really wants something, it wins. Lasting change comes from training the elephant with habits and environment, not lecturing it. What's your elephant pulling toward?"},
{"title":"Quiet people bring something loud rooms miss","source_title":"Quiet","source_author":"Susan Cain","topics":["psychology","careers"],
 "body":"Workplaces and schools often reward the fastest talker, not the best idea. Many introverts think deeply, listen well and do great work alone, then need time to recharge. Good teams make room for both styles. Are you the type who recharges with people or away from them?"},
{"title":"Autonomy, mastery and purpose beat carrots and sticks","source_title":"Drive","source_author":"Daniel H. Pink","topics":["psychology","leadership"],
 "body":"Bonuses work for simple, repetitive tasks. For creative work, what keeps people going is control over how they work, the feeling of getting better, and a reason that matters beyond the paycheque. What would make your work feel like yours?"},
{"title":"The less you know, the more sure you feel","source_title":"Unskilled and Unaware of It (study)","source_author":"Justin Kruger and David Dunning","topics":["psychology","logic"],
 "body":"In a well-known study, people who scored lowest on tests of logic and grammar overestimated their performance the most, partly because the skills you need to do well are the same ones you need to spot your mistakes. Learning a little often shows you how much you don't know. When did that hit you?"},
{"title":"Losses hit about twice as hard as gains","source_title":"Prospect Theory","source_author":"Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky","topics":["psychology","investing"],
 "body":"Losing $100 feels worse than winning $100 feels good, roughly twice as bad in many experiments. That's why we hold losing stocks too long and turn down fair bets. Knowing it lets you ask: am I avoiding this because it's bad, or because losing stings? Where does loss aversion get you?"},
{"title":"'If this, then that' plans actually get done","source_title":"Implementation intentions research","source_author":"Peter Gollwitzer","topics":["psychology","fitness"],
 "body":"'I'll work out more' rarely happens. 'When I get home from work, I'll put on my shoes and go for a run' happens a lot more. Tying an action to a specific situation hands the decision to your future self ahead of time. What's one if-then plan you could set today?"},
{"title":"We love what we help build","source_title":"The IKEA Effect (study)","source_author":"Michael Norton, Daniel Mochon and Dan Ariely","topics":["psychology","product-design","diy"],
 "body":"People valued furniture they assembled themselves, and origami they folded, more than identical items made by others, as long as they finished them. Letting customers or teammates build part of something makes them care about it. What have you built that you value way more than it's worth?"},
{"title":"Kids who waited for the marshmallow, revisited","source_title":"The Marshmallow Test","source_author":"Walter Mischel","topics":["psychology","coaching-youth"],
 "body":"Kids offered one treat now or two if they waited were followed for decades. Later studies found family background explains much of the link to success, but the kids who waited used clever tricks, like looking away or singing. Self-control is partly a skill you can learn. What's your trick for waiting?"},

{"title":"Calgary's bet on closed-loop geothermal","source_title":"Eavor Technologies","topics":["energy-tech","oil-gas","startups"],
 "body":"Calgary-based Eavor drills a sealed underground loop, like a giant radiator, and circulates fluid to bring up the earth's heat without fracking or water from the rock. It uses drilling skills Alberta already has, and it tested an early version near Rocky Mountain House. Could oil and gas crews power the next energy boom?"},
{"title":"Oilfield drilling tricks for clean heat","source_title":"Fervo Energy","topics":["energy-tech","oil-gas","startups"],
 "body":"Fervo borrowed horizontal drilling and fracturing from oil and gas to make geothermal work in places that weren't hot and wet enough before. Its Nevada pilot started sending power to the grid in 2023. Old skills, new energy. Who do you know whose skills could cross over like that?"},
{"title":"Lithium hiding in old oilfield water","source_title":"E3 Lithium","topics":["energy-tech","oil-gas","mining"],
 "body":"Deep salty water under Alberta's old oil fields contains lithium. Companies like Calgary's E3 Lithium are working on pulling it out with existing wells instead of big open-pit mines. The province could supply battery materials from infrastructure it already has. What else is hiding in what we call waste?"},
{"title":"Batteries made from salt instead of lithium","source_title":"Sodium-ion batteries","topics":["energy-tech","evs"],
 "body":"Sodium is everywhere, in salt and seawater, while lithium is limited and pricier. Battery makers like China's CATL have started making sodium-ion cells. They hold less energy than lithium, but cost less and handle cold well, which suits storage and city cars. Would you buy a cheaper car with less range?"},
{"title":"Batteries that rust on purpose","source_title":"Form Energy","topics":["energy-tech","startups"],
 "body":"Form Energy stores electricity by letting iron rust and then reversing it. Iron is cheap and plentiful, and the batteries are designed to release power for about 100 hours, long enough to cover several windless days. What problem are you solving with cheap, boring materials?"},
{"title":"Pulling CO2 out of the air and turning it to stone","source_title":"Climeworks and Carbfix","topics":["energy-tech","conservation"],
 "body":"In Iceland, Climeworks uses fans and filters to capture carbon dioxide from the air, and Carbfix pumps it into basalt rock, where it turns into stone within a couple of years. It's still expensive and small, but it proves the process works. Should we fund it or focus on cutting emissions first?"},
{"title":"Steel made with hydrogen instead of coal","source_title":"Stegra (formerly H2 Green Steel)","topics":["energy-tech","startups"],
 "body":"Steelmaking is one of the biggest industrial sources of CO2 because it uses coal to strip oxygen from iron ore. A Swedish start-up is building a plant that uses hydrogen made with clean power instead, so the by-product is mostly water. Which other heavy industry is overdue for a rethink?"},
{"title":"Heat pumps move heat instead of making it","source_title":"The Future of Heat Pumps","source_author":"International Energy Agency","topics":["energy-tech","diy","real-estate"],
 "body":"A heat pump works like a fridge in reverse, moving heat from outside air or ground into your home. Because it moves heat rather than burning fuel, it can deliver several times more heat than the electricity it uses. Newer cold-climate models work in deep winter. Would one work where you live?"},
{"title":"Small nuclear reactors, built more like products","source_title":"Ontario Power Generation, Darlington SMR project","topics":["energy-tech","tech"],
 "body":"Instead of giant custom plants that run years over budget, small modular reactors aim to be built in a factory-like way and repeated. Ontario is building one of the first in the G7, at Darlington. If it works on time, others could follow. Where do you stand on nuclear power?"},
{"title":"Solar panels and crops on the same field","source_title":"Agrivoltaics research","topics":["agriculture","energy-tech"],
 "body":"Raising solar panels over farmland can shade some crops from heat stress and cut water loss, while sheep graze underneath and keep the grass down. Farmers can earn from both food and power on the same land. Would you put panels on your land or a family farm?"},
{"title":"Recycling old EV batteries into new ones","source_title":"Redwood Materials","topics":["evs","startups","mining"],
 "body":"Founded by a Tesla co-founder, Redwood recovers lithium, nickel, cobalt and copper from used batteries and scrap and turns them back into battery materials. Old batteries become a mine above ground. What's something you throw away that should be worth money?"},
{"title":"The solar cell that could top silicon","source_title":"Perovskite solar research","topics":["energy-tech","tech"],
 "body":"Perovskites are crystals that absorb sunlight well and can be made cheaply. Stacked on top of regular silicon cells, they capture more of the sun's spectrum and have set efficiency records. The catch is durability outdoors. If they last, panels could get much better. What tech are you waiting on?"},
{"title":"Fixing fridges and food waste ranked near the top","source_title":"Drawdown","source_author":"Paul Hawken (editor)","topics":["conservation","energy-tech"],
 "body":"When researchers ranked climate solutions by impact, some unglamorous ones landed near the top, like handling refrigerants properly and wasting less food, next to wind and solar. Big wins aren't always flashy. What boring fix in your field would make a big difference?"},
{"title":"Vertical farms learned an expensive lesson","source_title":"AeroFarms and the vertical farming shake-out","topics":["agriculture","startups"],
 "body":"Indoor vertical farms raised huge sums promising local greens all year. Several, including AeroFarms, went through bankruptcy when power costs and thin margins caught up. Lighting crops with electricity is costly; the sun is free. What's a hyped idea where the unit economics never added up?"},

{"title":"Everyone survived after the ship sank","source_title":"Endurance","source_author":"Alfred Lansing","topics":["exploration","leadership"],
 "body":"In 1915, Ernest Shackleton's ship was crushed by Antarctic ice. He kept 28 men alive for nearly two years through morale, routine and a desperate small-boat crossing to South Georgia. All of them made it home. The wreck was found in 2022. Who would you want with you when everything goes wrong?"},
{"title":"Inuit knowledge found the Franklin ships","source_title":"The Franklin expedition search","topics":["exploration","travel"],
 "body":"Franklin's two ships vanished in the Arctic in the 1840s. For over 160 years searchers came up short. HMS Erebus was found in 2014 and HMS Terror in 2016, helped by Inuit oral history that had described the ships' fate all along. Whose knowledge do we overlook?"},
{"title":"Amundsen won by learning from the locals","source_title":"Roald Amundsen's expeditions","topics":["exploration","leadership"],
 "body":"Before the South Pole, Amundsen spent winters with the Netsilik Inuit while crossing the Northwest Passage, learning fur clothing and dog sledding. In 1911 those skills got his team to the Pole and back safely. Learning from people who already live it beats theory. Who's your teacher?"},
{"title":"A square filter in a round hole","source_title":"Apollo 13","topics":["exploration","space","leadership"],
 "body":"When an oxygen tank exploded on the way to the Moon in 1970, carbon dioxide began building up in the lifeboat module. Engineers on the ground built a fix out of things the crew had on board, including tape and cardboard, and walked them through it. All three came home. What's the best fix you've improvised?"},
{"title":"A 1977 spacecraft still phoning home","source_title":"Voyager 1","source_author":"NASA","topics":["exploration","space"],
 "body":"Launched in 1977 with 1970s computers, Voyager 1 became the first spacecraft to reach interstellar space in 2012, and engineers keep coaxing data out of it from billions of kilometres away. Built to last five years. What have you built that outlived its plan?"},
{"title":"The bottom of every ocean","source_title":"The Five Deeps Expedition","source_author":"Victor Vescovo","topics":["exploration","travel"],
 "body":"The deepest point on Earth, the Challenger Deep, was first reached in 1960. In 2018 and 2019, an investor turned explorer piloted a submersible to the deepest point in each of the five oceans. Much of the deep sea is still unmapped. Would you go down?"},
{"title":"Turning back is part of climbing","source_title":"Into Thin Air","source_author":"Jon Krakauer","topics":["exploration","hiking","leadership"],
 "body":"In 1996, several climbers died on Everest after pushing past their turnaround time and getting caught by a storm. 'Summit fever', wanting the goal so badly you ignore the plan, isn't just a mountain problem. Deciding your limits before you start helps. When did you wisely turn back?"},
{"title":"Proving a raft could cross the Pacific","source_title":"Kon-Tiki","source_author":"Thor Heyerdahl","topics":["exploration","travel"],
 "body":"In 1947, Heyerdahl and five crew sailed a balsa-wood raft from Peru to Polynesia in about 100 days to show such a trip was possible. Experts doubted it would float that long. Sometimes you test an idea by just doing it. What would you build to prove a point?"},
{"title":"The explorer who saw nature as one web","source_title":"The Invention of Nature","source_author":"Andrea Wulf","topics":["exploration","conservation","great-books"],
 "body":"Alexander von Humboldt climbed volcanoes and travelled through South America around 1800, and came back seeing nature as an interconnected system. He was among the first to warn that cutting forests changes the climate. More places are named after him than almost anyone. What connections do you see that others miss?"},
{"title":"Mapping the West from Rocky Mountain House","source_title":"David Thompson's surveys","topics":["exploration","hiking"],
 "body":"Fur trader and surveyor David Thompson mapped huge stretches of western Canada and the northern US in the late 1700s and early 1800s, much of it on foot and by canoe, working out of posts like Rocky Mountain House. His maps were used for decades. Where's your favourite place in the Rockies?"},
{"title":"A helicopter on Mars flew 72 times","source_title":"Ingenuity Mars Helicopter","source_author":"NASA","topics":["exploration","space","robotics"],
 "body":"Ingenuity was a tech demo planned for up to five flights. In 2021 it made the first powered flight on another planet, then kept going, flying 72 times over almost three years. Small experiments can outperform their brief. What side project became your main thing?"},
{"title":"The telescope parked 1.5 million km away","source_title":"James Webb Space Telescope","source_author":"NASA, ESA and CSA","topics":["exploration","space"],
 "body":"Webb sits far beyond the Moon, shaded from the Sun, and sees in infrared, which lets it look at some of the earliest galaxies and at the atmospheres of planets around other stars. Canada built its guidance sensor. What do you hope it finds?"},

{"title":"The tiny animal that survives almost anything","source_title":"Tardigrade research","topics":["biology"],
 "body":"Tardigrades, or water bears, are under a millimetre long. When things get bad they dry out and curl into a dormant 'tun', and in that state they've survived freezing, high radiation and even exposure to open space. Then add water and they wake up. What's your tun state?"},
{"title":"Most of an octopus's brain is in its arms","source_title":"Other Minds","source_author":"Peter Godfrey-Smith","topics":["biology","mind"],
 "body":"About two-thirds of an octopus's neurons are in its arms, which can taste, feel and partly act on their own. Octopuses solve puzzles and escape tanks, yet evolved intelligence completely separately from us. It's the closest thing to meeting an alien. What animal fascinates you?"},
{"title":"Trees share through underground fungus","source_title":"Finding the Mother Tree","source_author":"Suzanne Simard","topics":["biology","conservation","hiking"],
 "body":"A Canadian forest scientist found that trees connect through fungal networks around their roots, and argued that older trees pass carbon and nutrients to younger ones. How much they 'share' is still debated, but forests are more connected than they look. Next hike, what will you notice?"},
{"title":"Gene editing became a real treatment","source_title":"CRISPR and Casgevy","topics":["biology","tech"],
 "body":"CRISPR lets scientists cut DNA at a precise spot, and its developers won the 2020 Nobel Prize. By late 2023, the first CRISPR-based therapy was approved, for sickle cell disease. Tools that were lab tricks a decade ago now treat patients. What should we use gene editing for, and not use it for?"},
{"title":"You're carrying trillions of microbes","source_title":"I Contain Multitudes","source_author":"Ed Yong","topics":["biology","cooking","fitness"],
 "body":"Your gut holds trillions of bacteria that help digest food, train the immune system and may even affect mood. They feed on fibre, and a more varied diet tends to grow a more varied community. You're less an individual than an ecosystem. What would you change in your diet if you thought that way?"},
{"title":"Every animal lives in its own sensory world","source_title":"An Immense World","source_author":"Ed Yong","topics":["biology","dogs"],
 "body":"A dog smells the history of a lamppost, a bat hears its way through the dark, and some birds may sense magnetic fields. Each animal experiences a slice of reality we can't. Spending time imagining their world makes ours feel bigger. Which animal's senses would you borrow for a day?"},
{"title":"The salamander that regrows its limbs","source_title":"Axolotl research","topics":["biology"],
 "body":"Axolotls can regrow lost legs, and parts of their heart and spinal cord, without scars. Scientists study them to learn why humans heal with scar tissue instead, hoping to unlock better repair one day. They're also critically endangered in the wild. What would you regrow if you could?"},
{"title":"Evolution, watched live for decades","source_title":"Long-Term Evolution Experiment","source_author":"Richard Lenski","topics":["biology","logic"],
 "body":"Since 1988, a lab has grown 12 populations of E. coli, freezing samples along the way, for tens of thousands of generations. One population evolved a whole new ability to feed on a nutrient E. coli normally can't use. Patience turns small changes into big ones. What would you track for 30 years?"},
{"title":"Spider silk, tougher than steel by weight","source_title":"Spider silk research","topics":["biology","startups"],
 "body":"Some spider silks are stronger than steel for their weight and remarkably stretchy. Spiders can't be farmed, so start-ups have tried making silk proteins with microbes instead. It has proven hard to scale, which is its own lesson about turning lab wins into products. What material should exist that doesn't?"},
{"title":"Penicillin needed more than a lucky discovery","source_title":"The story of penicillin","topics":["biology","entrepreneurship"],
 "body":"Alexander Fleming noticed mould killing bacteria on a petri dish in 1928, but little came of it for years. It took Howard Florey, Ernst Chain and their team, then wartime mass production, to turn it into a medicine. Discovery is one job; making it useful is another. Are you the discoverer or the builder?"},
{"title":"Exercise grows your brain","source_title":"Spark","source_author":"John J. Ratey","topics":["biology","fitness"],
 "body":"Aerobic exercise raises a protein that helps brain cells grow and connect, and studies link regular exercise with better focus, mood and memory. A workout is as much for your head as your body. Do you think better after you move?"},
{"title":"The bird that chases endless summer","source_title":"Arctic tern migration research","topics":["biology","travel"],
 "body":"Arctic terns breed in the far north and winter near Antarctica, so they see more daylight than any other animal. Tracking shows some fly tens of thousands of kilometres a year, adding up over a lifetime to several trips to the Moon and back. Where would you migrate to every winter?"},

{"title":"Sudbury's nickel came from space","source_title":"The Sudbury Basin","topics":["mining","space","exploration"],
 "body":"About 1.85 billion years ago a huge asteroid or comet hit what's now Northern Ontario. The impact helped create one of the world's richest nickel and copper deposits, mined for more than a century. Canada's mining history started with a cosmic accident. What's the strangest origin story you know?"},
{"title":"In a gold rush, sell the shovels","source_title":"The Klondike Gold Rush","topics":["mining","entrepreneurship","small-business"],
 "body":"Around 100,000 people set out for the Klondike in the late 1890s; few struck it rich panning. Many of the steadier fortunes went to people who sold supplies, outfitted gear, ran transport and fed the miners. Every boom needs suppliers. What's the picks-and-shovels play in today's booms?"},
{"title":"The scientist who unlocked Alberta's oil sands","source_title":"Karl Clark and the hot-water process","topics":["mining","oil-gas"],
 "body":"In the 1920s, Karl Clark of the Alberta Research Council worked out how to separate bitumen from sand with hot water. It took decades and huge investment to become an industry, but his idea underpins the oil sands today. Big things often start in a lab nobody's watching. What's being tested quietly right now?"},
{"title":"Finding diamonds by following tiny clues","source_title":"Fire into Ice","source_author":"Vernon Frolick","topics":["mining","exploration"],
 "body":"Geologists Chuck Fipke and Stewart Blusson spent years following trails of tiny indicator minerals across the tundra before finding diamonds near Lac de Gras in 1991, setting off Canada's diamond rush. Most people quit long before. How long would you chase a clue?"},
{"title":"The minerals every battery needs","source_title":"The Canadian Critical Minerals Strategy","source_author":"Government of Canada","topics":["mining","evs","energy-tech"],
 "body":"Lithium, nickel, cobalt, graphite and copper go into EVs, phones and grid batteries. Canada has many of them and is trying to build mines and processing at home instead of shipping raw ore overseas. The energy transition runs through mining. Where should the next mines go?"},
{"title":"Metal nodules on the deep seafloor","source_title":"Deep-sea mining and the Clarion-Clipperton Zone","topics":["mining","conservation","startups"],
 "body":"Potato-sized rocks rich in nickel, cobalt and manganese cover parts of the Pacific seafloor. Companies want to collect them for batteries; scientists warn we barely know the life down there and damage could last a very long time. Is it worth it?"},
{"title":"Bacteria that mine copper","source_title":"Bioleaching","topics":["mining","biology"],
 "body":"Some microbes break down the minerals in low-grade ore and release the copper locked inside. Mines use this to recover metal from rock that would be too poor to process with heat. Tiny organisms doing heavy industry. What other dirty jobs could biology take over?"},
{"title":"Mining asteroids is harder than it sounds","source_title":"AstroForge and early asteroid-mining ventures","topics":["mining","space","startups"],
 "body":"Some asteroids are packed with platinum-group metals, and start-ups have launched small probes to study them. So far, getting there, prospecting and bringing anything back has proven brutally hard and expensive. It's a long game. Would you invest in it?"},

{"title":"Shopify started as a snowboard shop","source_title":"Shopify's origin story","topics":["startups","small-business","software"],
 "body":"In Ottawa in the mid-2000s, Tobi Lütke wanted to sell snowboards online and found the store software terrible, so he wrote his own. Other merchants wanted it, and the tool became the business. Your own frustration is a product clue. What tool did you wish existed this week?"},
{"title":"Airbnb survived on cereal boxes","source_title":"Airbnb's founding","topics":["startups","entrepreneurship"],
 "body":"In 2007 two designers who couldn't make rent put air mattresses on their floor during a sold-out conference. Early on, they kept the company alive by selling election-themed cereal boxes. Ugly, scrappy starts are normal. What's the scrappiest thing you've done to keep something going?"},
{"title":"Slack came out of a failed game","source_title":"Slack's origin story","topics":["startups","software","product-design"],
 "body":"Stewart Butterfield's team built an online game called Glitch that didn't take off. The internal chat tool they'd made to work together was the valuable part, and it became Slack. Twice, actually: Flickr also came from a game. What side tool in your work could be the real product?"},
{"title":"Instagram won by cutting features","source_title":"Instagram's origin story","topics":["startups","product-design"],
 "body":"Instagram began as Burbn, a check-in app with lots of features that few people used. The founders noticed users loved one thing, sharing photos, and cut almost everything else. Simplifying beat adding. What would you remove to make your thing better?"},
{"title":"Canva heard 'no' over 100 times","source_title":"Canva's founding","topics":["startups","product-design"],
 "body":"Melanie Perkins started with a school yearbook design business in Perth, then pitched a bigger design tool to investors and was turned down again and again before raising money. Canva now has users all over the world. Rejection is data, not a verdict. What's your longest streak of noes?"},
{"title":"Spanx: $5,000 and no investors","source_title":"Spanx's founding","topics":["entrepreneurship","small-business","sales"],
 "body":"Sara Blakely sold fax machines door to door, cut the feet off her pantyhose and saw a product. She started with her $5,000 in savings, wrote her own patent application, and kept full ownership for years. You don't always need outside money. What could you start with what you have?"},
{"title":"Patagonia gave the company to the planet","source_title":"Let My People Go Surfing","source_author":"Yvon Chouinard","topics":["small-business","conservation","philanthropy"],
 "body":"A climber who forged his own gear built Patagonia on fixing things and not overselling. In 2022 the family transferred ownership to a trust and a non-profit so the profits fund fighting climate change. Business can be a tool for a cause. What would you build a company to fund?"},
{"title":"Duolingo's founder made you train computers","source_title":"Duolingo's origin story","topics":["startups","tech"],
 "body":"Luis von Ahn co-created reCAPTCHA, which used the puzzles people solved online to help digitize books. Then he built Duolingo to make language learning free and game-like. Same trick both times: turn small daily actions into something big. What habit could you turn into a product?"},
{"title":"Landing a rocket to make space cheaper","source_title":"SpaceX booster landings","topics":["startups","space"],
 "body":"Rockets used to be thrown away after one flight, like flying a jet once and scrapping it. In 2015 SpaceX landed an orbital rocket's booster for the first time, and reuse helped push launch costs down. Reusing the expensive part changes everything. What 'disposable' thing should be reusable?"},
{"title":"Drones delivering blood in Rwanda","source_title":"Zipline","topics":["startups","robotics"],
 "body":"Zipline started in 2016 by flying blood to clinics in Rwanda by drone, dropping it by parachute. Deliveries that took hours by road took minutes. Starting with a life-or-death use case proved the tech. What problem near you could a drone solve?"},
{"title":"WD-40 is named after its 40th try","source_title":"WD-40's origin story","topics":["small-business","diy"],
 "body":"In 1953 a small San Diego company was trying to make a formula to keep rust off. The name stands for Water Displacement, 40th attempt. It's now in garages everywhere. Most products people love took many tries nobody remembers. What are you on attempt number what of?"}
]$json$::jsonb, 'ai');
